              "text": "4:                    Programming Assignment 1: Distributing an Application∗\r\n6:             Due date: Report is due October 5, Demo dates the following 3 days\r\n8:Your task in this programming assignment is to develop a component-based distributed\r\n10:You can achieve a total of 100 Points on this assignment. That is the number of points\r\n11:reflects the weight of each of the deliverables within this assignment.\r\n15:This programming assignment is a Travel Reservation system, where customers can reserve\r\n16:flights, cars and rooms for their vacation. You can find more information about the client\r\n17:interface and explore the functionality in the clientUserGuide.pdf.\r\n40:Beginning with the starter code provided on myCourses, this programming assignment\r\n41:requires you to distribute the simple application in two ways: RMI and sockets.\r\n43:        This assignment is an adaption of the project of CSE 593 of the University of Washington.\r\n48:The provided sample code already implements a single-client, single-server system using\r\n49:RMI for communication. On the client side, an interactive console takes text-based com-\r\n50:mands (see clientUserGuide.pdf), parses the input and sends a message over RMI to the\r\n56:                             Client                         RM\r\n60:The first task in this milestone involves distributing the system without modifying the client.\r\n62:  1. Introduce a new intermediary server called the Middleware that sits between the client\r\n68:     entire interface – the Middleware will only call the flight-methods on the flight-\r\n71:  3. Decide how to handle customers – either with an additional server, through replication\r\n72:     at the ResourceManagers, or at the Middleware (which then becomes a type of\r\n75:  4. Add an additional function bundle to the Middleware which reserves a set of flights,\r\n78:All clients send requests to the Middleware server which are then distributed appropriately.\r\n82:                    Client\r\n83:                                             middleware\r\n84:                                               server                 RM\r\n87:                    Client\r\n110:1.2   TCP Sockets (50 Points)\r\n112:with the RMI distribution but at the end the final TCP implementation should also have a\r\n113:distributed architecture with a middleware.\r\n116:Re-implement your system using TCP sockets for communication between all layers instead\r\n117:of RMI. The client can remain blocking (i.e. it sends a request and waits for the reply\r\n118:before sending the next request). However, the Middleware must not block when it is\r\n119:waiting for the ResourceManagers to execute a request. That is, when the Middleware\r\n120:receives a request from the client, it forwards it to the corresponding ResourceManager and\r\n121:continues accepting client requests. When it receives a response from the ResourceManager,\r\n122:it messages the appropriate client. Similarly, the ResourceManagers should be able to\r\n128:   • If you use AI for this part of the assignment then you MUST present a solution where\r\n130:     used for all (or at least nearly) all methods that can be called by the client.\r\n135:   • You can do this part of the assignment as team coding together, or you could decide to\r\n136:     have one person develop the initial solution and the other person perform the testing\r\n142:\f2    Report (15 Points)\r\n144:    • Write a short (3-5 pages) report detailing your architecture and design for both RMI\r\n145:      and TCP. The TCP section will likely dominate the report, and should explain your\r\n146:      strategy for message passing as well as concurrency. Briefly mention your implemen-\r\n150:    • One page of the report should list the tests you conducted to test whether your\r\n174:As last section, your report must contain a detailed description of the contributions of each\r\n175:of the group members. That is, for each of the tasks listed above (RMI distribution, TCP\r\n176:sockets, custom functionality and report), indicate how much and what each of the team\r\n194:“scrolling through the report”. You might also think of how to show particular test cases.\r\n200:client and servers. If you are not prepared at the time of the demo, the TA has less time to\r\n"
