          "value": "**Evaluating middleware replication**\n\nExisting RMI middleware performs replication calls across server instances, but the new TCP requires dedicated resources for RM and client operations. When replicating `newCustomer`, all RMs should receive the same reservation, and this means merging customer bills and deleting customers while releasing reservations. That aligns with the RMI approach, but I noticed `newCustomer()` can generate duplicate random IDs, creating a bug. A better middleware solution could generate sequential IDs for consistency across all RMs, using AtomicInteger while testing existing IDs.\n\n**Assessing middleware resource handling**\n\nImportant resources require that updates for the same flight are sent to the authoritative instance. The middleware should route special requests grouped by operation, which can indeed involve parallel, concurrent resource managers. The client uses the `TcpResourceManagerClient` stub class that may open a new socket connection for each request. To ensure safety, I think it’s better to maintain a persistent TCP session with a request/response reader thread. However, I have to consider UI call blocking and reconnection retries, so keeping one socket per operation to ensure correlation with request IDs seems ideal.\n\n**Analyzing middleware operations**\n\nMiddleware is handling operational dispatch by mapping resource operations like flights, cars, and rooms to their respective backends. There’s a concern about rolling back partial changes reliably, particularly with existing bundles. I’m considering whether a new bundle might reserve sequentially and roll back previously created reservations. However, this could lead to inconsistent states if pre-existing reservations are deleted. I might need to rely on separate resource managers and add checks before reserving resources. The interface also lacks a cancellation feature, which complicates things further.\n\n**Considering response aggregation**\n\