anagement**\n\nI'm thinking about using async state with callbacks here. Maybe it could be simpler to rely on a monotonic ID but keeping the static counter positive, starting from 1. I should also manage manual ID collisions with retries. My plan includes implementing a `newCustomer` fanout along with a single aggregate callback; if any response is false, I'd clean up only the successes and try again. To avoid reentry after cleanup, I'll create async operations through a helper, and the fanout registry should handle the result mapping.\n\n**Considering reservation flow**\n\nI’m thinking about whether a bundle must require at least one flight and how the user guide mentions at least one. I should ensure the customer exists across all options. Maybe I can preflight by querying customer info for available resources and prices, but I need to roll back if the reservation fails. The sequence for reservations focuses on targeting compensation actions, and I should track these successfully within the BundleContext’s actions list.\n\n**Considering local launcher implementation**\n\nI need to address the issue with the skeleton having empty hostnames. It's failing right now, so I'm thinking about implementing a convenient local launcher to help fix this. The user specifically wants a server setup, so I should make sure to add a script called run_servers.sh, which will orchestrate the server using tmux. This way, everything should work together more smoothly!\n\n**Updating README for TCP**\n\nI'm focusing on updating the README to include TCP directions, run instructions, and tests, in addition to what’s already there for RMI. I think it's important to ask the user input on significant design aspects, even though this mapping from the spec seems reasonable. I should check if raw Java is available by using `javac -version` in bash. Also, it might be helpful to validate the test code and inspect the report pages criteria before implementing around 10 files.",
