          "value": "## Big picture\n\nThe TCP version is a three-layer system:\n\n```text\nConsole client\n     │ TCP request / response\n     ▼\nMiddleware\n     ├── persistent TCP connection ──► Flights ResourceManager\n     ├── persistent TCP connection ──► Cars ResourceManager\n     └── persistent TCP connection ──► Rooms ResourceManager\n```\n\nThe assignment’s key concurrency requirement is that the middleware should not sit idle waiting for a ResourceManager response. This implementation gives each backend connection a response-reader thread, keeps a map of outstanding requests, and returns to accepting other client requests while those requests are in flight.\n\nThe main design choice is to keep the existing `IResourceManager` interface and common reservation logic, and replace the RMI communication path with TCP. The client’s existing command interpreter is reused rather than reimplemented.\n\n## What the tagged launch script does\n\n[`run_middleware.sh`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/Server/run_middleware.sh) is a small launcher:\n\n1. `set -eu` makes it stop on a failed command or unset variable.\n2. It resolves the script’s own directory, so it works independently of the directory from which it was invoked.\n3. It builds the TCP project with `make`.\n4. It starts `Server.TCP.TCPMiddlewareServer`, forwarding any arguments to it.\n\nWith no arguments, the middleware connects to `localhost` on the default backend ports. You must start the three ResourceManagers first; the middleware connects to all three during startup.\n\n## How one operation travels through the system\n\nFor example, when the user enters `AddFlight,100,5,125`:\n\n1. The inherited client command loop parses the text and calls `IResourceManager.addFlight(100, 5, 125)`.\n2. [`TcpResourceManagerClient.java`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/Client/Client/TcpResourceManagerClient.java) wraps that call in the common `invoke(method, arguments...)` helper. It opens a TCP connection to the middleware, sends a request, and waits for its response.\n3. The middleware accepts the connection and reads the request. It routes `addFlight` to the Flights ResourceManager—not Cars or Rooms.\n4. The middleware sends the request over its persistent Flights connection. It records which client should receive the reply, keyed by a middleware-generated request ID.\n5. The Flights ResourceManager’s dispatcher calls the existing `ResourceManager.addFlight`.\n6. The result travels back over the persistent backend connection. The middleware matches the response ID to the pending request, writes the result to the client’s socket, and closes that client connection.\n7. The client decodes the result and the existing command loop prints the success or failure message.\n\nThe client is synchronous from the user’s perspective: it waits for each command’s response before prompting for the next one. Multiple clients can still make requests concurrently.\n\n## The TCP protocol\n\n[`TcpProtocol.java`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/Server/Server/TCP/TcpProtocol.java) defines the shared wire format. It uses Java’s typed `DataInputStream` and `DataOutputStream`, rather than Java object serialization.\n\nA request contains a request ID, a method name, an argument count, and typed arguments. Supported values include integers, booleans, strings, and vectors of strings—the types used by the client API, including bundle flight lists.\n\nA response contains the request ID, an indication of success or failure, and either a typed result or an error message. Request IDs are important because responses can arrive asynchronously and need to be associated with the right waiting client. The middleware uses its own backend request IDs; the client’s request ID is preserved for the reply to that client.\n\n## Middleware behavior and concurrency\n\n[`TCPMiddlewareServer.java`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/Server/Server/TCP/TCPMiddlewareServer.java) handles the middle layer:\n\n- It accepts clients continuously and hands each accepted connection to a client-handler pool.\n- It keeps one persistent TCP connection per ResourceManager, with one reader thread per connection.\n- It stores in-flight requests in a concurrent pending-request map. When a backend response arrives, its ID identifies which pending call to complete.\n- It has routing rules for resource-specific operations: flight methods go to Flights, car methods to Cars, and room methods to Rooms.\n- Operations concerning customer state are sent to all three ResourceManagers, then combined or checked before responding.\n\nBackend requests are sent without waiting for the ResourceManager to finish. The middleware’s response-reader threads process backend replies independently of its client accept loop. This is the central difference from the incomplete draft, where stream handling and request/response coordination were not working as a complete protocol.\n\n## ResourceManager behavior\n\n[`TCPResourceManagerServer.java`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/Server/Server/TCP/TCPResourceManagerServer.java) listens for TCP connections. A connection handler reads requests and schedules each one on a request worker pool, so requests can run concurrently instead of forcing one operation at a time per server.\n\n[`ResourceManagerDispatcher.java`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/Server/Server/TCP/ResourceManagerDispatcher.java) maps protocol method names to calls on the existing common `ResourceManager`. It checks argument counts and types and reports unsupported methods explicitly. The inventory and reservation operations continue to use the shared implementation in [`ResourceManager.java`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/Server/Server/Common/ResourceManager.java), keeping transport code separate from business logic.\n\nState is in memory: each ResourceManager starts with empty inventory and customer state when launched.\n\n## Customers and bills\n\nThis follows the replicated-customer approach also used in the existing RMI middleware:\n\n- A manually requested customer ID is created on all three ResourceManagers. If registration only succeeds on some managers, the middleware attempts to delete those partial registrations.\n- For `newCustomer()` the middleware generates an ID, registers it everywhere, and retries if that ID already exists on any manager.\n- `deleteCustomer` is sent to all managers. Since each one stores the customer’s reservations relevant to its resource type, each can restore the inventory it owns.\n- `queryCustomerInfo` gathers the three manager replies, merges their reservation entries, and combines their totals. If no manager has the customer, the result is the empty string, matching the common ResourceManager behavior.\n\n[`Customer.java`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/Server/Server/Common/Customer.java) formats the bill and computes each reservation’s total as quantity times price.\n\n## Bundles and rollback\n\nThe middleware handles bundles as a sequence of individual reservations: all requested flights, then the optional car and room. On success, it returns `true`.\n\nIf one reservation fails, it sends compensating `cancelReservation` requests for the reservations that it knows already succeeded. For example, if a flight reservation succeeds but the requested room is sold out, the middleware cancels the flight reservation before replying that the bundle failed. The cancellation method is internal to this TCP implementation; it uses the existing ResourceManager reservation data.\n\nThis is compensating rollback, not a distributed transaction. It cannot guarantee atomicity if a process or network connection fails at a particularly unfortunate point—for example, after a reservation succeeds but before its response reaches the middleware. It also does not lock all three ResourceManagers as a single unit while a bundle is underway.\n\n## Starting it locally\n\nBuild and run the tests:\n\n```sh\ncd TCP\nmake\nmake test\n```\n\nStart four processes, in separate terminals:\n\n```sh\ncd TCP/Server\n./run_server.sh Flights\n```\n\n```sh\ncd TCP/Server\n./run_server.sh Cars\n```\n\n```sh\ncd TCP/Server\n./run_server.sh Rooms\n```\n\n```sh\ncd TCP/Server\n./run_middleware.sh\n```\n\nThen start the interactive client:\n\n```sh\ncd TCP/Client\n./run_client.sh [middleware_host [middleware_port]]\n```\n\nThe default ports are Flights `3101`, Cars `3102`, Rooms `3103`, and middleware `3042`. The middleware launcher accepts backend addresses in Flights/Cars/Rooms order, followed by its listening port. For example:\n\n```sh\n./run_middleware.sh flights.example:3101 cars.example:3102 rooms.example:3103 3042\n```\n\nThe client connects to the middleware only; it does not need to know the ResourceManager addresses. These instructions are also in the project [`README.md`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/README.md).\n\n## Tests\n\n[`TCPIntegrationTest.java`](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/TCP/tests/Server/TCP/TCPIntegrationTest.java) starts all three ResourceManagers and the middleware on ephemeral ports, then exercises the client against them. It checks:\n\n- Creating customers, rejecting duplicate IDs, and generating an ID.\n- Adding inventory, extending an existing flight, and querying counts and prices.\n- Reserving flights, cars, and rooms; blocking deletion while reservations exist; and releasing inventory when a customer is deleted.\n- Merging a customer bill and computing its total.\n- Successful bundle reservations and rollback when a requested resource is unavailable.\n- Concurrent inventory updates, concurrent work at the same ResourceManager, and the ability to complete another request while one flight query is deliberately delayed.\n\nThe suite passed. One environment note: this workspace has a Java runtime but not the standard `javac` executable, so I compiled and ran the suite with a temporary Eclipse Java compiler. Normally, `make test` invokes `javac`.\n\n## Relationship to the RMI version\n\nThe [RMI ResourceManager](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/Server/Server/RMI/RMIResourceManager.java) and [RMI middleware](/home/arman/Desktop/CS/TemplateClaude/Server/Server/RMI/Middleware.java) communicate through remote method calls and RMI registry bindings. In the TCP path, the client still calls the same interface, but `TcpResourceManagerClient` turns those calls into protocol messages; the middleware routes them explicitly; and the dispatcher invokes the shared ResourceManager implementation.\n\nSo the business operations remain familiar, but TCP makes the message format, routing, request correlation, and concurrency responsibilities explicit.",
